{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_de_http_backends_express_expressbackend", "label": "ExpressBackend", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "The right pick when the team already has Express middleware investments or is migrating an existing Express app incrementally; roughly 40K\u201360K req/sec, slower than Fastify.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/de/http/backends/express.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_de_http_backends_express_expressbackendoptions", "label": "ExpressBackendOptions", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "withApp lets an existing Express app carry the actor-ts routes alongside legacy ones, which is also how trust-proxy and TLS are configured.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/de/http/backends/express.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_de_http_backends_express_middleware_chain", "label": "Express-Middleware-Kette", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "The middleware ecosystem is the main reason to choose Express over Fastify; without that need Fastify is simply faster, and Express's own chain carries more overhead than Fastify's hooks.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/de/http/backends/express.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_de_http_backends_express_websocket_handshake", "label": "WebSocket-Handshake durch die Express-App", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "A websocket route registers as an ordinary GET, so app.use gates the handshake too; a middleware that answers aborts the upgrade, and the rejection body only reaches the client on runtimes that allow writing to a taken-over upgrade socket \u2014 Bun and Node do, Deno does not.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/de/http/backends/express.mdx",